  publisher = {Springer},
  year      = {2026},
  pages     = {605--618},
  doi       = {10.1007/978-3-032-29723-5_35},
  abstract  = {With the growing use of Artificial Intelligence (AI) and Large Language Models (LLMs) in software engineering, it is important to understand their impact on software security and how developers with varying levels of experience use them. This work explores the relationship between developers' levels of experience and the security of their code when using an AI tool (GitHub Copilot). We designed and conducted a within-subject user study involving novice and experienced developers, in which participants completed two programming tasks, one with access to Copilot and one without. We found that most participants wrote insecure code, regardless of their levels of experience. GitHub Copilot helped avoid certain types of vulnerabilities, yet it generally did not affect code security for both novice and experienced participants. We also found that the two groups differed in how they use the AI tool when writing code.},
}

